, "R. norvegicus", "Rn", "brown rat", "Norway rat"], "taxId": "10116", "abbreviation": "RNO", "className": "Species", "schemaClass": "Species"}, {"dbId": 48898, "displayName": "Bos taurus", "name": ["Bos taurus", "B. taurus", "Bt", "bovine", "cattle", "cow", "domestic cattle", "domestic cow"], "taxId": "9913", "abbreviation": "BTA", "className": "Species", "schemaClass": "Species"}], "isChimeric": true, "category": "transition", "className": "Reaction", "schemaClass": "Reaction"}], "literatureReference": [751038, 8982701], "reviewed": [{"dbId": 782850, "displayName": "D'Eustachio, P, 2010-05-21", "dateTime": "2010-05-22 03:10:45", "className": "InstanceEdit", "schemaClass": "InstanceEdit"}], "revised": [{"dbId": 9012014, "displayName": "Varusai, Thawfeek, 2017-07-10", "dateTime": "2017-07-10 16:30:32", "className": "InstanceEdit", "schemaClass": "InstanceEdit"}], "species": [48887], "summation": [{"dbId": 750990, "displayName": "G alpha z (Lounsbury et al. 1991) and G alpha 12 (Kozasa & G...", "text": "G alpha z (Lounsbury et al. 1991) and G alpha 12 (Kozasa & Gilman, 1996) are excellent in vitro substrates for all three subtypes of protein kinase C (PKC). Activation of PKC in intact platelets by agents such as thrombin, thromboxane A2 (TXA2) analogues and phorbol esters leads to rapid and near-stoichiometric phosphorylation of G alpha z (Carlson et al. 1989). The primary phosphorylation site is Ser-27 (Lounsbury et al. 1993). This phosphorylation blocks the interaction of G alpha z with Gbeta:gamma suggesting that it is a regulatory mechanism for attenuating signalling by preventing subunit reassociation.", "literatureReference": [{"dbId": 751014, "displayName": "Protein kinase C phosphorylates G12 alpha and inhibits its interaction with G beta gamma", "title": "Protein kinase C phosphorylates G12 alpha and inhibits its interaction with G beta gamma", "journal": "J Biol Chem", "pages": "12562-7", "pubMedIdentifier": 8647866, "volume": 271, "year": 1996, "url": "htt